<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Theagenes of Megara</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 640–620 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Theagenes was tyrant of Megara in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in the second half of the seventh century BCE. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> he won power by championing the poor against the rich, slaughtering the flocks of wealthy landowners. He built a fountain house in the city and supported his son-in-law Cylon's failed attempt to become tyrant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> around 632 BCE.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Everything known of Theagenes comes from anecdotes recorded by authors writing several centuries after his death, and their reliability is uncertain. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> gives two accounts of his rise: in one Theagenes persuaded the Megarians to grant him a bodyguard, which he then used to seize the city; in the other he came to power by slaughtering the flocks of the rich beside a river.</p><p>Modern historians treat both stories with caution. The bodyguard is a stock element in ancient accounts of how tyrants took power, and the meaning of the slaughtered flocks is disputed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> saw the episode as a tyrant siding with the poor masses against the rich aristocracy, but recent scholarship has suggested this reading may be anachronistic and has questioned whether such a conflict existed in archaic Megara. Greg Anderson sees the act as part of Theagenes' effort to assert his dominance over the Megarian aristocracy, while Thomas Figueira has proposed that it reflects a religious dispute over the ownership of sacred flocks.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> records that Theagenes married his daughter to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cylon-of-athens">Cylon of Athens</a>, an Olympic victor, and supplied the force with which Cylon, after consulting the Delphic Oracle, seized the Acropolis during the Olympic Games. Cylon and his followers were besieged and killed. Some scholars link this failure with Theagenes' own fall. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> says only that he was overthrown and exiled, without explaining how, and that Megara was afterwards ruled by an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oligarchy">oligarchy</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The geographer Pausanias credited Theagenes with a fountain in Megara and the long underground aqueduct that fed it, which he called worth seeing for its size, ornament and number of columns. Archaeologists have uncovered this fountain, but the surviving building dates to the early fifth century BCE and cannot be his work, although an earlier structure may have stood on the same site.</p><p>Although very little is known of his life, Theagenes is counted among the archetypal early Greek tyrants. He is also mentioned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristophanes">Aristophanes</a>' comedy Peace, where the chorus persuades Trygaeus not to sacrifice a fat swine, lest they be associated with the swinishness of Theagenes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Theagenes of Megara” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Orthagoras founded a dynasty at Sicyon, <b>Theagenes of Megara</b> seized power in his city, and in 636 or 632 BCE the Olympic victor Cylon of Athens, Theagenes’ son-in-law, failed in an attempt to seize the Acropolis.</blockquote><a href="/government/tyranny">Tyranny · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Theagenes</b> ruled as tyrant c. 640 BCE and backed his son-in-law Cylon's failed coup at Athens.</blockquote><a href="/state/megara">Megara</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Megara, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38,23.34&amp;year=-630" aria-label="Megara on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5132 798 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Theagenes of Megara"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5582 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5582 1068h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38,23.34&amp;year=-630">What was here in 630 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
